      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Detailed Deconstruction of the Five Core Pillars of UPS</h2>
        <p>
          The Unified Pension Scheme is codified around five statutory pillars designed to address every phase of superannuation:
        </p>

        <div class="pillar-cards-grid">
          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Pillar 1: Assured 50% Pension</h4>
            <p>Guarantees exactly half of the average basic pay drawn in the final 12 months for employees completing 25 years of regular qualifying service. For service between 10 and 25 years, the pension scales proportionately without arbitrary forfeiture.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Pillar 2: Guaranteed ₹10,000 Floor</h4>
            <p>Protects operational staff, Multi-Tasking Staff (MTS), and late-entry personnel by establishing an absolute statutory floor of ₹10,000 per month on superannuation after completing a minimum of 10 years of service.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Pillar 3: 60% Family Pension Safety</h4>
            <p>In the event of an employee’s demise, their nominated spouse or eligible dependent family members are guaranteed 60% of the pension drawn by the employee immediately prior to death, complete with Dearness Relief indexation.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-card">
            <h4>Pillar 4: AICPI-IW Dearness Relief</h4>
            <p>Neutralizes inflation by granting biannual Dearness Relief increases on the assured pension, family pension, and minimum pension, benchmarked to the All India Consumer Price Index for Industrial Workers exactly like working staff.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Additional Lump-Sum Superannuation Grant Explained</h2>
        <p>
          One of the least understood yet financially lucrative provisions of the Unified Pension Scheme is the <strong>standalone lump-sum payment at superannuation</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under traditional OPS rules, an employee receives their commuted pension advance and statutory retirement gratuity. Under the UPS, the government created an additional terminal payout that <strong>does not reduce the quantum of the monthly assured pension</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The Lump-Sum Superannuation Formula:</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Lump-Sum Exit Payment</strong> = (1 ÷ 10) × [Monthly Pay + DA on Date of Superannuation] × Completed Six-Monthly Periods (SMP)
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Example under 8th CPC Baseline:</em> Consider an employee superannuating after 30 years of qualifying service (60 completed SMPs) with a revised monthly basic pay of ₹1,20,000 (and 5% accrued post-implementation DA, making emoluments ₹1,26,000).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lump-Sum Payment</strong> = 0.10 × ₹1,26,000 × 60 = <strong>₹7,56,000</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            This <strong>₹7,56,000</strong> is paid directly to the retiree as a liquid cash credit at retirement, entirely separate from and <em>in addition to</em> their statutory retirement gratuity (capped up to ₹25L–₹35L), while leaving their 50% monthly assured pension completely intact.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Government Contribution Escalation: The 18.5% Dedicated Pool</h2>
        <p>
          Under the market-linked NPS framework, the employee contributes 10% of their Basic Pay + DA, while the Central Government remits 14%. 
        </p>
        <p>
          To underwrite the actuarial risk of guaranteeing fixed 50% pensions regardless of global market downturns, the Union Government elevated its budgetary contribution under the UPS to <strong>18.5%</strong>:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Individual Pension Corpus (10% Employee + 10% Government):</strong> Functions as the personalized asset pool invested across approved sovereign and corporate debt instruments.</li>
          <li><strong>Separate Central Guarantee Reserve (8.5% Government):</strong> Routed into a dedicated sovereign risk pool managed under PFRDA oversight. This pool bridges any shortfall between an individual's market returns and the legally guaranteed 50% benchmark at the time of retirement.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Consequently, the employee assumes zero market risk: if equity markets boom, the corpus is secure; if capital markets crash, the sovereign reserve pool ensures the 50% monthly pension arrives on the first day of every month without deduction.
        </p>
      </section>

